<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/intermediate/how-doctors-think">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Clinical diagnosis is <strong>applied probability under uncertainty</strong> — reasoning from
    symptoms to causes with incomplete information, where errors have consequences. Jerome
    Groopman's <em>How Doctors Think</em> catalogued the cognitive traps; evidence-based
    medicine built the countermeasures. This lesson is the diagnostic method and its built-in
    biases.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the diagnostic loop, and the biases that bend it" widget="equationStep" config={dxCfg} />

  <h2>The arithmetic every clinician runs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sensitivity and specificity:</strong> how good is the test? A 99%-sensitive test still misleads when the disease is rare — positive predictive value collapses as prevalence drops (the Bayesian lesson's medical-trap example, applied constantly).</li>
    <li><strong>Occam and Hickam:</strong> Occam says find one explanation for all findings; Hickam counters that patients can have several diseases. Knowing when to merge and when to split is the craft.</li>
    <li><strong>Sick or not sick:</strong> triage thinking precedes diagnosis — the unstable patient gets treated before being fully understood.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnostic error is a top patient-safety problem:</strong> studies estimate diagnostic errors affect ~1 in 20 adults seeking outpatient care (Newman-Toker, 2023) — which is why checklists, decision-support tools and second looks exist.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Groopman, How Doctors Think (2007); Newman-Toker et al., BMJ Quality & Safety (2023); BMJ Best Practice methodology.</p>
</TopicPage>
